{
  "about": "Where `$$` display maths opens and closes, read the same on the phone (apps/mobile/lib/markdown/displayDollars.ts) and on the server (apps/api/app/modules/chat/display_dollars.py). remark-math's rule: a delimiter is a run of exactly two unescaped dollars (three or more, or one in brackets, is a price tier); at the start of a line (after its indent, a quote mark or a list marker) it opens a formula that may run on over the lines below; mid-line it is maths as $$x$$ with nothing between the dollars and the formula (any script: $$π$$), or as a padded $$ x = 1 $$ whose text holds a maths mark (= < > ^ _ \\ |) and at most one word (\\text{…} groups and commands aside), closed on its line, or as a $$ that ends a sentence's line, whose formula lines run to a $$ that starts a line before a blank line (a line inside it like |x| < 5 or + 2x + 1 is maths); a $$ alone on its line, or with TeX after it, opens a block only its closer ends, while one with words after it or in a list item reads as prose, which a blank line, a list item or a table row ends unclosed; a pair on a table row never spans a |. Inline code holds no maths, and a single $ pairs with no dollar beside another and around no letter or digit, so a one-line price tier holds no inline maths either ($–$$ is a range: apps/mobile/lib/markdown/inlineMath.ts, apps/api/app/modules/chat/math_padding.py). `display` lists each formula's text between its dollars, trimmed, in order. The phone's tests (lib/__tests__/displayDollars.test.ts, displayDollarsWiring.test.ts) read every case with the reader, convertBlockMath, displayMathLines, the inline readers and the streaming scanners; the server's (app/tests/modules/chat/test_display_dollars.py) with its reader, display_math_lines, the inline-boundary and calculation readers and the LaTeX check.",
